{"timestamp": 1778701761.4938853, "duration_sec": 2.914677858352661, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "They really\u2014 they really only come when they have to."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "The reason they don't like it is because of the immigration, the way they treat them when they're crossing. They always get harassed. Yeah. When they're going back to America, they get harassed. So they get tired of that."], [1, "Los Algodones has long been set up for the benefit of Americans, and the journey Americans take to cross into Molar City has always been a simple one. But of course, the journey in the opposite direction is much more complicated. Can I ask you a question that might sound\u2014 I don't know, I don't know how you'll take it, but I'll ask it anyway. You can tell me how you react to it. I've heard from Americans and Canadians, like people describing what it's like to go down to Los Algodones, and people describe just walking freely through the border, like just 5 minutes, no need to even show a passport. And I, I'm just curious what that's like for you on your end, knowing that you were kicked out, and yet part of your job is to interact daily with I don't know, hundreds of people who just walk so freely across this border, you know?"], [3, "The way I see it is like this. Since I am from the border and we lived in America, we went to school in America, what happens is that when I see people coming from America and I see myself that I can't go back, which is like literally Right there, I can see my mom's house from right here."], [1, "Oh, wow."], [3, "That's the hardest thing to see. I see my house. I got the Colorado River, and I can literally see the water tank at the reservation, and then I can see all the way to the Yuma hospital on 24th Street, and then I kind of know more or less where my mom's house is over here. If I had a drone, I could fly it over there and and look at and go, \"Ouch.\"."], [1, "Yeah."], [3, "That's how close I am. That's not easy. It's very hard for me."], [1, "It's 5:00 AM in Algodones. The sky is still dark, not quite sunrise, and Alberto is shuffling around the house, getting ready for for work. The streets are mostly quiet at this hour, just the sound of other promoters and vendors setting up, setting the stage for Molar City. By 6:00 AM, he's at his post. The border opens and the Americans descend. They walk in the shadow of the 30-foot wall that divides them. Crossing the very threshold Alberto cannot."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["immigration", "border crossing", "harassment", "deportation", "mexican american border", "los algodones", "americans in mexico", "travel restrictions", "family separation", "daily work routine", "border wall", "emotional hardship", "dentistry tourism", "indigenous reservation", "colorado river", "inequality"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778701853.8115237, "duration_sec": 2.0192513465881348, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, 100%. It's a matter of time. But, I mean, I will say, my son, even tonight getting out of the truck, he was like, I really love this truck. Oh, okay, good. That's really good. So he's forgetting about the Cybertruck. Maybe. But, I mean, we still see Cybertrucks all the time, so it's like, yeah. He's happy. He's happy, though. Well, hopefully we can take him off-road on the rodeo, see what he thinks then. Yeah."], [0, "Jay's in chat. He's asking, do we get the $7,500 tax credit? Well, that's now..."], [0, "Available for new Cybertrucks. Yes. All right. So for us, well, we no longer have Cybertrucks. But for you, Jay, you got yours last year. No, you do not qualify for it. Now, I do hear that if you have it under your business, that you can do something there. I've heard about that, too."], [0, "So go talk to a CPA is what my recommendation would be my recommendation. We can't give him any official advice, but I will confirm that I've heard some things too. No, no. Yeah. So if you have a business, you're using it for business reasons, talk to a CPA is what I would say."], [0, "and let you know youtube so you know you could see oh see now jay's saying the 179 yes jay that's a random number yeah section 179 100 uh if you don't know what that is once again go see your cpa ask him"], [0, "Tell him you got a Cybertruck and you want to explore section 179. And I'm sure he will know exactly what that is. And he will walk you through whether or not you can use that. Yeah. Needed it. So I wanted to, I want to talk about some cool, you know, we're always talking about superchargers and,"], [0, "supercharging locations and the evolution of superchargers. And so one thing that actually Will sent us this week was the fact that Tesla and Steak and Shake are working together. There's not really many Steak and Shakes out my way. Actually, there's a map down here that you can see right here."], [0, "There's not very many near me. There's a couple. I'm a bigger fan because obviously in the middle of that big sieve red, that's where I grew up. I'm a huge fan of 6G. Perfect, right? They're going to be building a Tesla supercharging stalls at steak and shake locations."], [0, "as you can see here, there's already six sites signed already with 20 plus sites in design review. And as you can see, the official stake and shake X account says, uh, thank you, Tesla charging. Let's do over a hundred locations. Consider all sites approved. So a stake and shake is fully in with Tesla and wants to get these, um,"], [0, "you know, superchargers locations at their, you know, restaurants. So that's really cool. That's something that, you know, because I've complained about that before, about how a lot of superchargers, a lot of just EV charging stations are always kind of like in the back corner somewhere in a weird parking garage or something. You got to walk a distance to get to, you know, something. And, you know, just being able to park,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cybertruck", "electric vehicles", "truck ownership", "off road driving", "tax credit", "business deduction", "section 179", "cpa advice", "tesla", "superchargers", "charging stations", "steak and shake", "automotive industry", "tax policy", "restaurant partnerships", "retail parking lots", "travel convenience", "infrastructure development"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778702125.0580592, "duration_sec": 1.66575288772583, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "My goodness, I mean, what's harder than wrestling? I don't think there's anything."], [1, "The hardest sport in the world. Yeah. The hardest sport in the world and the best sport in the world to get your kids into at a young age because the discipline and the mental toughness that they get will carry them through for the rest of their life."], [0, "Yep. Tenacity, the, just the stick-to-itiveness, whatever you want to call it. Yeah. That's just like. Even high school wrestling."], [1, "I remember wrestling in high school and I had already done martial arts, but I was like, I'd never trained that hard. I was like, I can't believe. And then it carried me over into my Taekwondo career because I realized like, I was like, oh, I'm leaving a lot on the table. Like, I'm not training like these guys are, right? So I started running, I started adding all these things to my training that I wasn't doing before. I started doing a lot more calisthenics, a lot more different things. I'm like, I'm leaving something on the table because we were not training in the gym and we were sparring hard, we were doing hard rounds, you'd get tired, but it was not the same as what we were doing in wrestling. No one trains harder than that."], [0, "Aerobic stuff, man, that's, that's, man, I got, I got the wrestling bug when And when I was in my\u2014 I was a senior in high school, and the football coach was a wrestler in college, and he challenged me. I think we did this 2 years in a row, in my junior year and my senior year. At the end of the year, we'd wrestle. We'd just go like, you know, he and I. Like I said, I was big for my age. Were you playing football? Uh, I was for a very short time, but I ended up, um, I was, uh, I wasn't, I wasn't designed for, for, um, team sports. Me neither. And I ended up beating up the football coach."], [1, "Oh no."], [0, "Yeah, I had a, dude, like, I had, I had the worst temper than anybody I'd ever seen. I mean, I used to go into fits of rage. I was so angry early on, man. It's like the Hulk is like, Mike, you should chill out a little bit, man. It's like I was just\u2014 It's probably."], [1, "From being on your own at 14."], [0, "Yeah, you know what it is, is like I was growing up in a very harsh environment, and I was\u2014 I didn't know I was an artist. I didn't know I was a writer, director, whatever. You know, they didn't\u2014 you didn't see those growing up where I am. And so when you're a sensitive kid, man, what you do is you build armor. Like, I was to play Mike Tyson later on, and I understood him quite well. And if you're sensitive, you, you know, anything that's precious, you put it in, you lock it in a safe, and you become the safe. And it's, it's like, I grew up, my brothers were completely different. They're engineers. So things rolled off their back. But like, for me, just\u2014 I was just volatile. And luckily, I had martial arts to kind of put my focus into. But like I said, like I was to play Mike Tyson, I understood him a great deal. And, you know, even though you take the moniker of this monster, it's only to hide what's really deep inside. And that's why you would see, if anybody's gonna go into tears in front of a million people, it's people like Mike Tyson. And you go, \"How does that fit in the same person?\" And so, that's what I was growing up. And, you know, I don't know if you know this, but I was a schoolteacher before I was an actor. Oh, really? Yeah, I taught EMDR. I was a special ed teacher. So I focused on a lot of kids who were very much like me. And I still do that. In a way, I consider that my real job. Whenever I'm off from work on a movie or whatever, I go into the inner cities. I go into community centers. I devote my time because there's nothing that I could do\u2014 there's no better spending of time than something like that because I was luckily, luckily saved. I had just at the right times in my life just different seeds planted.. And so I'm, I'm confident that if those seeds were not planted, I would not be here. Because like I say, I was, I've been through, uh, some, some crazy stuff."], [1, "It's a classic story."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["wrestling", "martial arts", "high school sports", "training intensity", "discipline", "mental toughness", "tenacity", "taekwondo", "conditioning", "calisthenics", "anger management", "special education", "trauma", "emdr therapy", "inner cities", "community centers", "acting career", "personal growth"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778702232.7611406, "duration_sec": 1.7212212085723877, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Hey, everyone, and welcome to Developer Tea. My name is Jonathan Cottrell, and today I'm answering a question from Matt Johnson. Matt is a listener of Developer Tea. He's been listening for quite a while, and he actually asked me a series of questions, all of which I think are going to be very interesting to you. But I wanted to share a little personal story with you. I actually just got back"], [0, "from a long endeavor into the country of Ireland. My wife and I took a vacation to Ireland. It was an incredible trip. We loved it. I'll probably end up posting a series of photos over the next couple of weeks on my Twitter account. You can follow that and see my completely unrelated to development pictures of our romping through Ireland and"], [0, "at developer t or at jcattrell depending on the day some days i will tweet more on one or the other just kind of depends on my mood for that day i do tweet regularly from developer t episodes that i have done in the past as well as the newest episode for the day and"], [0, "So follow developer T on Twitter to stay up to date. And that is also a place where you can ask me questions. Now, Matt actually asked me a question by emailing me at developer T at Gmail. And that is how he asked such a long and detailed question. Matt is in an interesting situation. So I'm going to go ahead and read Matt's question."], [0, "Hi Jonathan, I came across Developer Tea while looking for web development podcasts and have greatly enjoyed many of the episodes. Your constant encouragement to contact you finally got the best of me. The reason I'm writing you is to hopefully get a bit of advice from someone knowledgeable in the development field. I'm at a point in my life where I am looking to learn web development for a future career and wanted to get your advice on if you think my plans and goals are realistic or not. Let me give you a little background."], [0, "My current situation is that I have a long-term health issue that makes me unable to work a traditional 9 to 5 job and an office. I had to quit my previous job, which I was trained for, and got my degree in because of this. Now I am in the position of trying to find a new career that can be done remotely from home. I have always been interested in web design and development, but have never pursued it further than passing interest because of lack of time."], [0, "Now that my situation has changed, I've been looking at it seriously as a possible future career and have become excited about the idea of learning web development. Well, Matt, you're in the right place. A lot of people listening to this podcast are in the process of learning how to be a web developer."], [0, "With that history in mind, my questions for you are as follows. Matt had four questions for me today. First of all, is it realistic to be able to learn the skills needed to be a web developer through an online school or online bootcamp? The short answer is no."], [0, "And that kind of comes as a surprise to a lot of people probably because I have online schools and online boot camps that sponsor this show and I'm a big believer in them. But the truth of the matter is that the skills that you need to be a web developer come by you practicing web development. Certainly an online school or online boot camp is a great place for you to begin learning those skills and"], [0, "But is it enough for you to get all of the skills you need? No. Is it something that I recommend? Absolutely. There are many different ways to go about instruction, whether it's self-instruction or pseudo-guided instruction, like if you were to go to an online boot camp,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["web development", "web design", "online learning", "coding practice", "career change", "remote work", "health issues", "online bootcamp", "online school", "software skills", "mentorship", "podcasts", "distance education", "self instruction", "professional development", "workforce training", "decision making", "digital nomadism"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778702726.414506, "duration_sec": 1.9034509658813477, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "is not inherently a sign that you're not making the right choice. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. I agree. I agree. Yeah. Well, thank you. I think you gave us a lot of food for thought today. So is that anything else that we missed that you'd like to add? Like I said, we're open source. That means we're a monorepo, so you can go and"], [0, "Look at all I've talked about. If you go to our website and use the link to GitHub there, you can see all that. And I will say, so I'm proud of the code in there, but I also am realistic in that there's a lot of sharp edges. There's a lot of stuff in there that works because I know how it works."], [0, "and it works for me and somebody coming in they might be like well that's a strange decision and i and i do know there's places where oh it breaks down here and here's where you got to be a little little careful in using it so i i'm not going to say this is like production ready for other people it's definitely production ready for us yeah well and nobody will be able to criticize your code about the json parser because you already said that yeah it's like"], [0, "But the reality is that in any software system, you end up with parts of the code that are not the best code you could possibly write for a variety of reasons. Yeah. I mean, going back to the experience breeds wisdom idea is I remember being young and looking at other people's code and being very critical of it. And now that I'm there, I'm like, all right, well, I know why those decisions were made. And sometimes those are the right decisions."], [0, "I had to pay beers to colleagues once criticizing some code I saw. I was really upset with them, only to find out that it was the code that I wrote a few months earlier. I think many of us make this mistake. Yeah, yeah. Okay, thank you very much, Malcolm, for coming to the show. It's been a pleasure. Thank you very much. This is Giovanni Asproni for Software Engineering Radio. Thank you for listening."], [0, "Thanks for listening to SE Radio, an educational program brought to you by IEEE Software Magazine. For more about the podcast, including other episodes, visit our website at se-radio.net. To provide feedback, you can comment on each episode on the website or reach us on LinkedIn, Facebook, Twitter, or through our Slack channel at seradio.slack.com. You can also email us at team at se-radio.net."], [0, "This and all other episodes of SE Radio is licensed under Creative Commons License 2.5. Thanks for listening."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["open source", "monorepo", "github", "code quality", "production readiness", "software systems", "technical debt", "code criticism", "developer experience", "code review", "maintenance", "software engineering", "ieee software", "podcasting", "social media", "creative commons", "communication", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
