w Uses for Office Buildings: Life Science, Medical and ...\nThe author conducted a review of recent publications and market data on office conversions and interviewed developers, architects and other commercial real estate professionals to provide an overview of the key considerations that go into converting an office building to a life science, medical office or multifamily use."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": null}, "prompt_chain": null, "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["commercial real estate", "real estate development", "property development", "housing policy", "multifamily housing", "office buildings", "office conversion", "adaptive reuse", "building renovation", "life sciences", "medical office", "urban development", "urban planning", "transportation funding", "mortgage lending", "banking", "credit unions", "digital banking", "mergers and acquisitions", "retail leasing", "food halls"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703194.1960537, "duration_sec": 1.576009750366211, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Was on Friar Street."], [1, "Yep, yep, yep."], [0, "Right down from the Goosens."], [1, "Yep. Yeah, right down."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "And, um, that, that was an honor, man, to be able to train in that gym. That was incredible. It was incredible."], [0, "Oh yeah, yeah, I used to be there. Yeah, yeah, man. Yeah, those are some great times because, I mean, I connect with Benny where\u2014 because when I was in Bridgeport, my instructor Manny Malice went to California and started training with Benny. Oh wow, they're early on. So he put that on the map about coming to the mecca and training with Benny."], [1, "It was the mecca for kickboxing, especially in the '90s. That was the mecca. You had to go to the Jet Center."], [0, "Everyone talked about the Jet Center. Yeah, man, that was\u2014 So we were."], [1, "Always wondering, like, what is the style? What's the best style? And then the UFC came along and I'm like, oh my God, they did it. They did it. They figured it out. They put it all together. And for a while it was jiu-jitsu because nobody understood jiu-jitsu and Royce Gracie was just running shit."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["kickboxing", "training", "gym", "martial arts", "jet center", "california", "bridgeport", "instructor", "fighting styles", "ufc", "jiu jitsu", "royce gracie", "sports history", "combat sports", "travel", "competition", "athletic culture", "innovation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703407.1233635, "duration_sec": 2.2888669967651367, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But for a person that's never seen\u2014 barely seen $1,000 anywhere, anything with million, they're gonna be excited about and take it before."], [1, "Exactly, exactly. And over time, most people are not gonna win, so most people are dumping money into it."], [0, "There was a story of a young lady, I don't know exactly what it was, I think she won some type of lottery where they gave her two options. She would get, I think it was like a payout of like $2 or $3 million right up front, or they give her, I think it was like $20,000 every month for as long as she lived."], [1, "Yeah, that's all they did."], [0, "And she did this. Well, people think that it's kind of crazy, but if you consider the fact that she was probably 20, 21, her life expectancy probably\u2014 she was white, so she probably lived to 132. You know what I'm saying? She looked like\u2014 that was just smart. A lot of people would not understand that. That was a smart thing."], [1, "But I did\u2014 I don't think it's for the rest of your life. I think it's until it reaches that number. I don't think they're going to give you money for the rest of your life."], [0, "Well, maybe I was reading the headline."], [1, "Maybe it's a different kind of lottery."], [0, "That I'm not aware of. Maybe. I think it was something as long as she lives."], [1, "That sounds crazy. Yeah. All right, here it is. Typical Mega Millions jackpot run total ticket revenue is usually several times the advertised jackpot, but there's no single fixed average because sales vary enormously with the jackpot size. Still, you can get a good ballpark. So around 50% of ticket revenue goes into the overall prize pool. So the government makes 50% right off the bat, right? So if it's a $100 million payout, they already made $100 million. So that's $200 million is what they made. They throw in $100 million for everybody. Of that prize pool, roughly two-thirds to three-quarters is allocated to the jackpot, with the rest funding lower-tier prizes.. So that means even if there's $200 million, out of the $100 million, only two-thirds of it goes into the big jackpot. And that means the jackpot is typically in the order of one-third of total ticket sales that run. And then out of that one-third, so say if it's $100 million, or with the Epstein case, it was $80 million. He took the payout, which was $30 million. So they make $50 million on top of that. And then on top of that, you pay taxes on that $30."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["lottery winnings", "lump sum payout", "annuity payments", "mega millions", "jackpot size", "ticket sales", "prize pool", "government revenue", "payout options", "tax payments", "financial literacy", "gambling odds", "public finance", "probability", "risk management", "behavioral economics", "wealth planning", "income stream"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703582.5739133, "duration_sec": 4.227331638336182, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nLibraries, Education, and\nNeighborhoods Committee\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nWednesday, April 22, 2026, 9:30 AM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nThis meeting also constitutes a meeting of the City Council, provided that the meeting shall be conducted as a\ncommittee meeting under the Council Rules and Procedures, and Council action shall be limited to committee\nbusiness.\nPlease Note: Times listed are estimated\nA. Call To Order\nMeeting Start Time: 9:32 a.m.\nPresiding Officer: Councilmember Rivera\nPresent: 5 - Maritza Rivera, Joy Hollingsworth, Dionne Foster, Eddie Lin,Alexis Mercedes Rinck\nB. Approval of the Agenda\nC. Public Comment\nD. Items of Business\n1. Appt 03492 Appointment of Quynh Pham as Director of the Department of\nNeighborhoods, for a term to December 31, 2029.\nThe Appointment (Appt) was discussed.\nE. Adjournment\nMeeting Adjournment Time: 10:15 a.m.\nPrepared by: Nicholas Duda\nPage 1"], [0, "Squeezed between high costs and low revenue, small businesses are taking on debt, putting off bill payments and laying off staff as they struggle to stay afloat. - Facebook\nJan 15, 2026 \u00b7 Downtown Seattle: Often 35\u201339%+ (e.g., nearing 35% in Q2 2025, up to 39.1% in some Q4 2025 data, with submarkets like Pioneer Square over 50%). Missing: districts | Show results with: districts"], [1, "How Small Town High Streets Are Outpacing City Centres in Retail ...\nUrban high streets , once dominated by department stores and large chains, now face widespread closures and vacant spaces. However, small towns are experiencing a retail renaissance driven by localism, community engagement, and innovative retail formats. What\u2019s fueling this\u2026"], [2, "Home -- EuroShop - World\u00b4s No. 1 Retail Trade Fair - Next event: Feb...\nExperience EuroShop 2026 live: Discover global retail trends , expert insights and innovations shaping the industry. Stay ahead and explore the highlights now!"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "libraries education and neighborhoods committee", "seattle city hall", "seattle", "councilmember maritza rivera", "joy hollingsworth", "dionne foster", "eddie lin", "alexis mercedes rinck", "quynh pham", "department of neighborhoods", "nicholas duda", "council rules and procedures", "facebook", "downtown seattle", "pioneer square", "euroshop", "euroshop 2026", "urban high streets", "city centres"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703643.649711, "duration_sec": 1.9042136669158936, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "most probably your IDP topic will never come because it is linked. GitHub's first approach, then I generally build an IDP on the top of it. I mean, to me, with a service catalog, you're still starting off with a service. And for a service, you'll need some kind of API interface that you want to define and share. Of course. Or a spec you want to share. So if a team has that, let's say, gRPC or an OpenAPI spec or a REST spec, right? Is that sufficient or is it still a long way to go before that becomes part of your service catalog?"], [0, "No, it could be really simple, like the landing zone. Everybody is speaking about landing zone. It's a simple thing. But behind this landing zone, you could have multiple aspects about the security, the governance, the cloud provider, and all this stuff. So"], [0, "Of course, you want to have at the end some stacks with security, network, infrastructures, application layer that is as flexible as possible, right? But a simple use case, which is the base of everything that we are deploying a landing zone or the set of tools that we have, right? For example, I don't know, as a developer, I need to get access to my"], [0, "10 tools as a developer to develop, right? Or VDI topic, right? Or scaffolding some new project. It's not something complicated, but takes some time. You can easily build some stacks around this without, you know, overthink about which kind of service catalog. It's things that you're using all the time. So I would say the most simple one is landing zone and tools that the developer are using. Yeah."], [0, "And one thing you mentioned that's interesting was security. How do you see security or lack of play a role in both the adoption and friction against IDP? What we see in terms of security is that security is also taking the path of security as code. Like we saw premium software, whatever, everything as code. Jego policy and stuff like this. So there is this huge need of"], [0, "a GitOps first approach. Of course, security, like at the time, we're more focusing on compliances and, you know, bundle and software plus hardware, right? But we see that with the rise of cloud, there is more and more security as code. And as soon as you have this approach as code, you can embed it everywhere, inside your pipeline, inside your stack, inside your policy management, everywhere, right? But"], [0, "More we are diving in the, I would say, the cave of infrastructures, more the ASCOD is less obvious, let's be honest. But what we see and what we didn't see a couple of years ago is that this initiative can even come from the security team that came, you know, or the network team, for example, that..."], [0, "want to give access to everything that they have done in a simple way, because they know that they will never understand the clue about everything that we are designing. Because it's a word, like the security aspect, the network aspect. You can't scratch it, or you understand what happened, or you're in the matrix. So we see some teams that are coming on this path that go as code and go for a self-service approach."], [0, "to give access to everything that they have done, respecting best practices, which is GitHub's first design approach, right? This sounds like there is still a bit of legwork that teams have to do to adopt a security as code culture, right? But for, and I'm going to go on a limb here, for a big proportion of the population that is not adopting this culture, how do you get them over that hurdle so they can be IDP ready? I mean..."], [0, "Our conviction is that you should respect the borders and the competencies of each business unit. And your platform should be capable to include the complexity of this big organization. And this is one of the key adoption, right? For example, on our side, we have some forms."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["internal developer platform", "github", "service catalog", "api design", "openapi", "grpc", "rest api", "landing zone", "developer tools", "security", "security as code", "gitops", "governance", "compliance", "cloud computing", "self service", "devops pipelines", "organizational change"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703666.7604136, "duration_sec": 1.887740135192871, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Listen, this ransom note fiasco is what happens behind the scenes on so many cases. This is not unique. What's unique about it is that everybody's hearing about it and watching it play out really beautifully."], [0, "you know real time because it's in this case but these people are extortionists they are pathetic uh they prey on these families and and pray for money but listen came in three days late they"], [0, "did not target the family. They went straight to the media when they weren't getting the media play they hoped to get. They went to TMZ, finally got the media play they hoped to get. I really don't think this is going to be connected. And I don't think this ever had anything to do with money. This had to do with torture, torture family."], [0, "Let me ask, okay, why? What's the motive here? What is the motive? What are your thoughts? This is what these people do. It happens behind the scenes so often. It's unbelievable. They even get the person's voice."], [0, "sure use that they send out uh ransom demands with the people's faces this is not unusual sure i guess my i think maybe i didn't phrase my question right i'm not i'm not asking why i get why i mean some horrible actor would be like hey let's try and you know get some money and torture this family and you know but"], [0, "I guess my question more so is if all of these ransom notes are simply noise, and we're really talking about two completely separate cases, take all the ransom out of it, take all of the videos that Savannah and her family has done begging and pleading and we're going to pay and all that, take it all out as if it never happened, as if it's not part of the case. We're just sitting over here left with someone who took Nancy from her home, but they have not asked for any sort of money. They have not done any sort of ransom note."], [0, "Is that an abduct? What is the purpose there then, I guess? What's the point? Why was Nancy taken if they're not going to ask for money, if they're not going to try and get something out of this? What the hell happened here? I see what your point is. Yeah. In my opinion, this was an abduction. And I always go back to who benefits from Nancy being gone. Yeah. Do you think they knew that this was Nancy? Do you think they knew specifically who they were targeting?"], [0, "Yes. Okay. That this wasn't some sort of, you know, B&E gone wrong. I mean, because the only, the other take I had on this is that this was a B&E, and you've known, B&E gone wrong. They freaked out. They'd rather not leave the body there than get charged with murder. If you can disappear the body and you never get caught, then, you know,"], [0, "It is what it is. You're able to get away with something horrible, maybe never intending for it to have gone that bad. But I think that's an interesting element. And I spoke to this last night on Court TV of if this is someone that"], [0, "that is not a sociopath it's not a psychopath that that for whatever reason you know thought that the best route to to get money to provide for one's family is b e is robbery is whatever because there's a lot of people who think that well this is the path to take uh and they somehow justified it in their mind that they're taking money from the rich old lady who's not going to miss it anyhow"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["ransom note", "extortion", "kidnapping", "abduction", "media coverage", "tmz", "motive", "torture", "family victim", "investigation", "burglary", "robbery", "criminal psychology", "organized crime", "crisis communication", "ethical reasoning", "true crime media", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778704637.9487045, "duration_sec": 5.97425103187561, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nLand Use and Sustainability Committee\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nWednesday, April 15, 2026, 9:30 AM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nThis meeting also constitutes a meeting of the City Council, provided that the meeting shall be conducted as a\ncommittee meeting under the Council Rules and Procedures, and Council action shall be limited to committee\nbusiness.\nPlease Note: Times listed are estimated\nA. Call To Order\nMeeting Start Time: 9:32 a.m.\nPresiding Officer: Councilmember Lin\nPresent: 5 - Eddie Lin, Dan Strauss, Dionne Foster, Joy Hollingsworth,Alexis Mercedes Rinck\nB. Approval of the Agenda\nC. Public Comment\nD. Items of Business\n1. CB 121195 An ordinance relating to land use and zoning; adopting interim provisions\nto expand the capacity of transitional encampments; and amending\nSections 23.42.054 and 23.42.056 of the Seattle Municipal Code.\nThe Council Bill (CB) was discussed.\n2. CB 121196 An ordinance relating to land use and zoning; rezoning cert"], [0, "Navigating Multifamily Lending: Rates, Cap Rates, and ...\nApr 15, 2026 \u00b7 Explore current multifamily lending market conditions, including agency rate changes, cap rate trends, and investment sales volume, for informed CRE financing decisions."], [1, "Rates Outlook: Expectations Versus Reality in the Multifamily ... Multifamily Cap Rates Poised to Slip in 2026 - blog.firstam.com Impact of Interest Rates on US Real Estate Development 2026 2024 Multifamily Outlook 2024 Multifamily Outlook 2024 Multifamily Outlook Multifamily | CBRE Multifamily Deals Face Headwinds Amid Interest Rate and ...\nOct 25, 2024 \u00b7 Explore how declining interest rates in the future could impact multifamily real estate , sales and construction, driven by the Fed's monetary policy. Nov 17, 2025 \u00b7 Multifamily cap rates may decline modestly in 2026 as fundamentals improve, credit eases, and demand stays steady, signaling a gradual, uneven recovery. Jan 30, 2026 \u00b7 See how 2026 rates affect US construction loans, ROI & refinancing. Expert analysis on the Fed's impact on multifamily and industrial development projects now. Is the multifamily market a good investment in 2023? The multifamily market may see additional strain from continuing high-levels of new supply and"], [2, "Multifamily Cap Rates Poised to Slip in 2026 - blog.firstam.com\nNov 17, 2025 \u00b7 Multifamily cap rates may decline modestly in 2026 as fundamentals improve, credit eases, and demand stays steady, signaling a gradual, uneven recovery."], [3, "Why NYC's Office - to - Residence Conversions Are Thriving Right Now...\nOffice - to - residence conversions don\u2019t just change buildings, they change neighborhoods. As more people move into formerly commercial zones , demand for retail, dining, and public services increases. This revitalizes local economies and supports small businesses."], [4, "Seattle - Wikipedia\nSeattle Pride Fest 2024. Seattle has a notably large lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender community. According to a 2006 study by UCLA, 12.9% of city residents polled identified as gay, lesbian, or bisexual."], [5, "Not every obsolete office building is cut out to become apartments\nResidential conversion offers some promise, but will not be a panacea for either the over-supply of offices , or the under-supply of affordable homes, because not every obsolete office building can be converted to housing."]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 132132