areer", "multiple jobs", "industry roles", "personal fulfillment", "life constant", "helping people", "free access", "audience", "content creation", "broadcasting", "digital media", "philanthropy", "professional identity", "community building"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778706915.5801654, "duration_sec": 2.5005693435668945, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Every day, thousands of people, mostly American, mostly white, and mostly retired, come to this town looking for relief from the turmoil in their mouths. Here, dental care costs up to 80% less than what it might cost in the US. Dental tourism to Los Algodones is so common that many call this place Molar City. Today, there are nearly 1,000 dentists in this 7,000-person town, and 98% of those in the patient's chair traveled here from outside Mexico. Lasha is going to take it from here."], [1, "Watch any one of these YouTube videos, and you'll see how a trip to Molar City is made easy for an American. For decades, this crossing didn't even require a passport. In one video, a retired couple arrives at Arizona's edge. They do a 360, displaying the massive lot where they've just parked. \"We love Mexico,\" they say emphatically, talking to the camera. The camera then pans to show the rust-colored border wall in front of them. That corrugated monstrosity. They marvel. Then a US Border Patrol vehicle starts driving past the screen, on guard, it seems, for anyone trying to cross in the opposite direction. To this, the camera woman speaks again, as if she's talking directly to Border Patrol. \"Get 'em, boys,\" she says. Then they begin their walk into Mexico. In Los Algodones, there are dense clusters of medical offices and a canopy of billboards above them. Vendors are set up on sidewalks where they sell handmade goods and kitschy souvenirs, like this one graphic tee that says, \"Keep calm, you're on the fun side of Trump's wall.\" And then there are the street promoters, people who are stationed at almost every street corner. Each of them competing for superlatives: the best deal, the quickest appointment, the most popular clinic in town. In Molar City, a street promoter is the first person a tourist will encounter."], [2, "Good morning, buddy."], [3, "What is it? Dental?"], [1, "Pharmacy?"], [3, "Glasses?"], [2, "I got dental promotion."], [1, "Some promoters try to lure people in with discounts. Others vie for attention in more creative ways. Like dressing up in a full-body tooth costume and waving a sign. Then there's Alberto, a street promoter who takes a different approach altogether. He tries to attract customers with humor."], [3, "I'm always trying to make stuff funny, you know, make them laugh like a comedian almost, make them relax just for that second."], [1, "Alberto is employed by a couple clinics in town. He's assigned to a specific street corner where he stands all day and tries to make his commissions."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["dental tourism", "los algodones", "mexico border", "american retirees", "dental care costs", "molar city", "dentists", "medical clinics", "street promoters", "tourism marketing", "youtube videos", "border crossing", "healthcare economics", "retirement travel", "humor", "sales commissions", "border security", "souvenirs"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778707297.8682425, "duration_sec": 2.0076212882995605, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So yeah, there's definitely a power to being able to overreact when everyone else is underreacting and also tone down overreactions when everyone else is overreacting when they shouldn't be. So it's, it's, it's been good. We've had a lot of freedom to do that."], [1, "Nice. So why don't we talk, uh, I'm going to cover quickly. We had some changes of course come out. We had Grizzled Company change, which, uh, they lost the real ones to wound. So my opinion on that is, I think when I on a WhatsApp team chat for Ignite, I said, well, that's actually a pretty big nerf. And I think I stand by that because the meta we're in right now, uh, is quite thick and what I would say narrow, right? Like this terminology of wide and narrows is something I use a lot. And narrow means armies are fielding a narrower amount of units, right? They, you know, typically between like 9 and 10 units. And the biggest one there is Necrons, obviously. And reroll 1s to wound is extremely good against Necrons. That's very, very impactful. And, you know, if you've got a Rogal Dorn that wants to shoot a C'tan on an objective, that is something that will happen every single time when you play that matchup. And that's really, really big. The main gun rerolling 1s and then 1 wound is massive. And in addition to that, Rough Riders, uh, which I was playing 3x5 of, that was my main, uh, key unit I think for early skirmishing because they get Lance and then they hit on 2s. So when you charge them, they hit on 2s rolling 1s with +1 to hit, and then they have Strength 9 Lance and they reroll 1s to wound. So if you're just going to try and kill, I don't know, a Rhino or something like that, it's winning on 3s rolling 1s, it's going 8B4 or 5 Marines, for example. It makes them super consistent, and I think that they are the biggest losers of the reroll 1s to wound, because often they only have 1 attack each. So, you know, you roll 5 attacks and you roll 2 1s, right? And then you're like, well, okay, damn. So I think the nerf is\u2014 the nerf is obviously\u2014 I think Grizzle Company is still a fantastic detachment, but I, I don't think\u2014 I think it's definitely the third on the top 3 now in terms of army strength. And I\u2014 that was enough to put me over the line going up to Manchester, saying I'd rather play Ultramarines than Grizzle Company Guard. And I was gonna play\u2014 I was playing Grizzle Company Guard beforehand, so that's what I was practicing for the tournament. And then this change has really put me over the edge. I said, you know what, I don't actually think I've got the strength to get through 4 rounds of probably playing Necron players, so I'm gonna have to go with something that's got to have a little bit more output. Particularly on these close quarters. So I went with Ultramarines, and that was actually a big deciding factor because the two of them were quite close. But I do think Ultramarines pips them now, and I do think it's very obvious that Necrons are the best army in the game right now by a pretty significant margin, I think."], [0, "Oh wow, really interesting."], [1, "Okay, you're really strong, man."], [0, "You mean at the top, top end, or you mean across the board?"], [1, "I think across the board. Doesn't matter what player skill you have, Necrons are the best army in the game. They are extremely easy to play. Yes, yes, you can play, you can play Pantheon of Woe, you can play Starshatter. I think Starshatter is the best take-all-comer list, and you can even play 40 Necron Warriors, which is extremely good right now as well, because the meta is obviously trying to kill C'tan, in my opinion. So yeah, it's, um, it's, it's an interesting one. Necron Masterclass coming out soon, obviously."], [0, "Uh, the\u2014 I've always been a Necron player."], [1, "Yeah, yeah. It's, um, it's a\u2014 yeah, it's a tough meta. I mean, how does Aeldari go into Necrons? Oh, it's just a slog as well, dude."], [0, "I can't believe you just said 40 Warriors and I felt the sweat develop on Aeldari players across the world. Our Discord just went silent for a second."], [1, "It's so hard."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Gaming"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["warhammer", "necrons", "ultramarines", "imperial guard", "grizzled company", "detachment rules", "nerf changes", "reroll wounds", "rough riders", "tournament prep", "meta analysis", "list building", "probability", "competitive gaming", "tabletop strategy", "discord chat", "game balance", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778707382.562332, "duration_sec": 1.9266026020050049, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And that reinforces even at a time where people are skeptical and there's so much partisanship that on this issue, the court had independence. It wasn't just a vote based on the party of the appointee or whether they had a history with Trump. Quite the contrary. Gorsuch may appreciate that Trump appointed him. They may like each other to some degree, but the law is the law."], [0, "And this wasn't a close call by the time it reached the high court. They'd lost in the lower courts. Trump did acknowledge the loss today. He complained. He lashed out. He showed a kind of anger maybe because he knew he lost in public at one point, complaining that he has other powers like embargoes or war powers. So he thinks it's unfair that if he has those other big powers, why should this whole tariff power be limited?"], [0, "Well, let me tell you something, and this is an easy thing to understand. That complaint is not a legal or even a logical argument. The whole point of limited and separate powers in our system, the reason why we're not like Putin's Russia or Stalin's Russia or many other countries you could think of, is that we separate the powers."], [0, "We have different powers in the government spread out. They're not all held by one person, let alone the king. And yet I'm going to let you hear, alongside the ruling, our president marveling at this system of checks and balances, almost as if he just learned about it today. I cannot charge them anything. Think of that. How ridiculous is that? I'm allowed to embargo them. I'm allowed to tell them you can't do business in the United States anymore. We want you out of here."], [0, "But if I want to charge him $10, I can't do that. It's incorrect. Their decision's incorrect. He's upset. He disagrees with it. He thinks it's incorrect. And yet Trump's presentation references the limits on his power as he conceded the limits. He said he'd look for other ways to pursue tariffs, meaning other ways not barred by the limit on his power under this court and longstanding law."], [0, "They merely overruled a particular use of IEPA tariffs. And essentially, it's to use to get a fee. It's ridiculous, but it's okay because we have other ways, numerous other ways. We have a totally firm decision now, and I don't think the court meant it because the court doesn't show great spirit toward our country, in my opinion. He's entitled to his opinion."], [0, "But the president was speaking as a man shown up here by a code he does not control. That's how power works. You can have some and still face other forces with their own power. That's true from the halls of government power in Washington out to the streets. Or as a classic simple moment captured it in the hit show The Wire, which explores power and its limits, law and its inconsistencies,"], [0, "You'll see how when one man experiences the limits on his supposed authority, he wants it to be one way. But it's the other. You want it to be one way. What? You want it to be one way. Man, I don't know what you... You want it to be one way. Ben, stop! Stop saying that. But it's the other way."], [0, "Trump wanted it to be one way, but sometimes it's the other way. Today, it was the other way. The Supreme Court was able to say, uh-uh. And this is a crucial setback for any parts of Trump's second term that involve him wanting to be a king or seize unitary autocratic powers or claim that emergencies mean he gets to do what no one else is allowed to do."], [0, "The learning curve may be tough, but it's the other way. We're going to get into that next. You want it to be one way. What? You want it to be one way. Man, I don't want it to be one way. Stop. Stop saying that. But it's the other way."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["supreme court", "judicial independence", "checks and balances", "presidential power", "separation of powers", "tariff authority", "emergency powers", "iepa", "trump administration", "lower court rulings", "constitutional law", "partisanship", "trade policy", "executive overreach", "political polarization", "autocracy", "television drama", "governance theory"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778707687.5206385, "duration_sec": 4.611677885055542, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "September 10, 2025\nA regular meeting of the City Council of the City of Boston was held in the Christopher A. Iannella\nChamber, City Hall on Wednesday, September 10, 2025, at 12:05 P.M.\nPresident Louijeune in the Chair. Present Councilors Breadon, Durkan, Coletta Zapata, FitzGerald,\nFlynn, Mejia, Murphy, Pep\u00e9n, Santana, Weber, and Worrell.\nBishop Homicil, Voice of the Gospel Tabernacle, delivered the invocation, and the meeting was opened\nwith the pledge of allegiance.\nThe Chair moved to adopt the minutes from the August 27, 2025, meeting.\nMotions prevailed.\n_________________________________________________________________________\n1619 Message and order for your approval an order to reduce the FY26\nappropriation for the Reserve for Collective Bargaining by Fifty-Five\nMillion Eight Hundred Ninety-Nine Thousand One Hundred Eighteen\nDollars ($55,899,118.00) to provide funding the Boston Public Schools\n(BPS) for the FY26 impact of increases contained within the collective\nbargaining agreem"], [0, "Boston City Councilors to Discuss Re-Legalizing Triple-Deckers\nBut the excitement evident among Boston City Council meeting Wednesday showed the door might be cracking open to re- legalizing three - and four -family buildings in many parts of the city."], [1, "Boston council hearing spotlights plan to legalize triple ...\nNov 18, 2025 \u00b7 Public testimony included residents who described triple deckers and ADUs as multigenerational housing lifelines; other commenters urged attention to fire\u2011access and setbacks and warned about institutional investors targeting single\u2011family lots."], [2, "CitizenPortal.ai - Boston councilors, police and retailers ...\nOct 17, 2025 \u00b7 Small-business witnesses and trade groups told the council that lost sales, rising security costs and damaged storefronts are threatening thin retail margins and neighborhood retail diversity."], [3, "Shoplifting rose 30% in Boston last year. What\u2019s being done ...\nFeb 9, 2025 \u00b7 Shoplifting in Boston rose 30% last year, according to city crime data. The spike was the primary driver behind a 2% increase in property crime, Police Commissioner Michael Cox said in..."], [4, "Prevent Theft in Retail - By Petrosoft\nImprove your c-store's bottom line. Trusted by thousands of US convenience stores. CStoreOffice back-office software overcomes your c-store hurdles. Request a free demo! Reduce Inventory Loss \u00b7 100% Remote Set-Up \u00b7 Increase C-Store Revenue \u00b7 Integrate with POS system Service catalog : Back-office solutions, Loss Prevention Analytics, SmartPOS"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["boston city council", "boston", "city hall boston", "christopher a. iannella chamber", "president louijeune", "councilor breadon", "councilor durkan", "councilor coletta zapata", "councilor fitzgerald", "councilor flynn", "councilor mejia", "councilor murphy", "councilor pep\u00e9n", "councilor santana", "councilor weber", "councilor worrell", "bishop homicil", "voice of the gospel tabernacle", "boston public schools", "police commissioner michael cox"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778707882.4964902, "duration_sec": 1.8522601127624512, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "No, I agree, actually. I really do. He's like, oh, people don't like it because of energy prices. Nobody's blaming AI for energy prices. Now, they're blaming some people for it. They're blaming data centers for it. Are they? I'm not. Oh, yeah, definitely. It's not even top of my list. Yeah, a lot of people blame data centers for it. That's why there's been so much pushback on them. And then the biggest reason why data centers are being built is because of AI. So it's like, you know."], [0, "I think Bill was just mad at him, and he's trying to deflect. That's what I think. I think he made a very poor PR move, and he's just trying to. Speaking of deflecting, Musk's latest interview, AI has entered its self-evolutionary cycle. I think we talked about that before. Why are we listening to a guy that is never right about anything he predicts?"], [0, "Well, he's right. I mean, it has entered itself. I mean, we talked about this. Anthropics using Claude to build Claude. I mean, it's not fully, but it's getting there. Fine, but why would we listen to him? He's never right about anything. He's never right about the things that he can even control. Full self-driving. Where's that? Where's that at? Where do you get that? I'll tell you this, man. I have..."], [0, "in full disclosure, ridden in a Tesla with a, with a friend that did the whole full self driving on all the side roads and all that stuff. And it is light years ahead of anything I've ever seen. It's not full self driving. I know it's not. No, you're right. It's not really full self driving, but its ability to navigate from my neighborhood, literally across Atlanta by itself is pretty wild. So, uh, anyway, fine moving on, but it's not full self driving. Yeah. Uh,"], [0, "Netflix, you know, they didn't get to buy Paramount. So now they're doing another thing. Netflix is buying Ben Affleck's AI startup. Not on my bingo card was the fact that Ben Affleck had an AI startup. It sold for like, what, $600 million, I think? Yeah, yeah, exactly. Because they were like, well, we got this money, burn a hole in our pocket. What are we going to do now? So apparently Ben Affleck started this company to do kind of filmmaking AI stuff. Yeah."], [0, "There you go. Okay. He's 600. I don't know how much of the company he owned, but he's at least a couple hundred million dollars. I mean, enough because he's still, like, advising with the company. So he was, like, actively engaged with the company, apparently, and he's still staying on and advising after selling it, I think. Yeah. Yeah, that might be because they just want their hooks in more with Ben Affleck. Yeah, maybe. Netflix had just had that movie, Ben Affleck and Matt Damon, together on there, and I totally blanked on the name. Yeah, that was the one. What was the name of it?"], [0, "Good Will Hunting? No, not Good Will Hunting. The fact that you were like, wait, what was it again?"], [0, "No, there's a new movie where there are cops. And it's like, it's not Good Will Hunting. It's a bust of a house. My favorite interview about Good Will Hunting was they interviewed Matt Damon. He was telling the story about how the network wanted to get it from an R rating down to a PG-13. There were too many F-bombs in it. And they were like, and the network, Matt was like, well, how many are in our movie?"], [0, "And how many can be rated R? They're like, oh, well, three. And he was like, well, we should probably be able to do that. Like how many are in our movie? And they were like, it was like 160 or something like that. And Matt was like, what? It was like, that's just the way we talked. Like we didn't even think about it. Like it wasn't even. Oh, that's so funny. So good. Oh, all right. It's got to take a little bit of work."], [0, "Yeah. Val Kilmer resurrected by AI to star in as deep as the grave. So I think, I think if I understood this correctly, he had already committed to the film. Yeah. And then he passed away. And so the film worked with his estate to get permission and worked with all of the, all of the acting agencies and all that, whatever they are. Yeah. Guilds, I think to get permission and about, we're going to have an AI Val Kilmer."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["artificial intelligence", "energy prices", "data centers", "public relations", "elon musk", "self evolution", "tesla", "autonomous driving", "netflix", "startup acquisition", "ben affleck", "filmmaking technology", "media industry", "corporate strategy", "movie ratings", "screenwriting", "actor likeness", "entertainment law"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778708455.77766, "duration_sec": 2.0346553325653076, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "played basketball for a little while. He's got great feet, great athlete, and it looks like he's got a lot of upside in the NFL."], [0, "Yeah, I was hoping they would pick up a tight end in this draft. You know a lot more than I do, Ben. But I've kind of felt like that was a really glaring weakness last year. And like a weakness that wasn't caused by injury. Like maybe a running back situation could have been a little better, but Dobbins was injured. This was just, we needed a player. So I hope these tight ends pan out, at least one of them."], [0, "Yeah, and they got two different skill sets. I mean, you got Jolie, who we already discussed, who's more of like a big-body receiver than a traditional tight end. And then the kid they got from Utah there with the pick right before Mr. Irrelevant. You know, he had no designs on even playing football. He was a guy who played like five snaps of high school ball. For a broken season, tibia and fibia, five snaps into his senior season. Didn't play another down a high school ball. Then he went on an LDS mission here to Colorado, and he was here for two years living in Parker."], [0, "And then after that, couldn't figure out what he wanted to do. His brother talked him into walking on at Snow College. And from there, that transferred to a scholarship at Utah. And now he's in the NFL. So interesting journey for him. Ben, you're so intelligent with all of this that I think we're forgetting to acknowledge some names. We keep saying who is playing. I think the earlier one you were talking about was Tyler Onyedem, the first pick, correct? And then did you mention Jonah Coleman? Because I thought his story was pretty fascinating of how his position came to be."], [0, "Yeah, I hadn't talked about him yet. That was the next pick they made, first pick of the fourth round that they had. Jonah Coleman's a running back out of Washington. He was at Arizona State prior to that. Now, I talked to his high school coach, and he said he's one of these great leader guys, always wants to be first one in, last guy out, hard worker. You always hear that as a cliche, but when you talk to guys all the way back to their high school days that are like that, it's great. We had Jonah on. We got a chance to interview him, and it's funny. He never started out playing running back. He was actually a wide receiver."], [0, "And the only reason he even became a running back is because his buddy, his best friend, was late to a game, so they stuck him in a running back. And the rest is sort of history. Kind of funny, though, that same buddy who was late to that game was actually sitting on the couch with him when he got the call from the Denver Broncos. He also tweeted that he's never been to an NFL game."], [0, "Correct, yeah. He's never been to an NFL game in his life, so his first game as a Bronco will be his first NFL game. That's an amazing story. You interview, over the years, lots and lots of drafted players. I'm curious whether..."], [0, "I'm projecting a little bit here. It seems to me like it would be really a lot of fun to interview some of these guys who were really late in the draft, maybe didn't expect to get drafted and suddenly they get the call. There's a whole different thing, and I'm not saying it wouldn't also be fun, but to interview some guy who was the second pick in the draft and everyone knew he was great. But can you talk about that dynamic from the perspective of doing your job?"], [0, "their whole life. That's fun. But you're right. There's a component to talking to the guys on day three, some of them who are not even expecting to get drafted. A couple of years ago, the Broncos drafted a guy named Montrell Washington, and Montrell was not expecting to get drafted. When he received the call from the Denver Broncos, he was in the drive-thru at Zaxby's ordering chicken."], [0, "And had to like mess up his order to like get out of the line and celebrate while he was being drafted by the Denver Broncos. And so, you know, there's there's something to I think that that sort of organic moment of joy of somebody who never expected it to sort of get that call and, you know, and find out they're going to be a professional football player and hear the stories behind that part of it."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["nfl", "denver broncos", "nfl draft", "tight ends", "running backs", "wide receivers", "player scouting", "athlete background", "college football", "injuries", "player interviews", "late round picks", "career development", "leadership", "lds mission", "fast food restaurants", "sports media", "underdog stories"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778708597.2014446, "duration_sec": 2.62568998336792, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And this can actually happen, I think, and I saw this happen when you have these sub-crates or whatever we call it, right? You have like a workspace with many crates inside, right? Something like that? Yes, exactly. Exactly. Basically, this is a multi-repo, okay? So in the Rust world, it's called the workspace, but it's basically a multi-repo where you have several projects"], [0, "that are somewhat related in the same repository. And you have to put in the repository link in each one of the TOML files, in each one of the sub-creates. And it's a little bit strange for me because the URL is the same for each one of them, right? So I think the repository link should be able to be taken from the top TOML file where you list the sub-creates."], [0, "Because now it's easy to forget what I'm saying, that you add a new crate inside the workspace and then you forget, someone forgets to add the repository because it's not added by default. And that's how it might easily happen that even for a company that has several crates with the repository link and then some without."], [0, "Right? That can easily happen. And it's sort of a little bit boring adding this link to every one of these sub-crates when I notice that that happens. And I thought about it already, but I haven't done that. I should probably go and see if this could be added to the main terminal file and the root of the repository. And if not, then maybe to open an issue on cargo to allow this, because"], [0, "This is the same information for each one of the crates so probably there shouldn't be any reason to replicate it there"], [0, "Yeah, I can see how annoying that would be. And you wouldn't want to copy and paste that, right? Some kind of way that you can write it once. That'd make much more sense. Yeah, that's what I'm doing now. It's annoying. There must be a way. If not, yeah. I'm also not surprised if there's no way. This is the point where people start writing some wrappers around Gargo to copy this automatically because they don't have it in the central one. Or someone adds this, the extra possibility to add to the central Tomloss file."], [0, "Let me take a look. You have crates without owner, which is interesting. So you can have a crate without an owner. I don't even know how that even works. Is that possible? I don't know. Maybe it's a bug in RustDigger. Okay. So there is the crates without owner name, but the crates without owner, that's the most surprising, right? I don't know what's going on there. I think I didn't have time to really try to understand what's going on there."], [0, "I actually opened an issue on crates.io, I think somewhere, asking about the database and some of the fields that I didn't understand, because there is a database dump that I'm using, and I wasn't clear about all the fields. I think I haven't received a response yet, but, well, time will..."], [0, "do the thing. Now, one thing you might have noticed that in each of these lists in the reports, under the reports, these are actually ordered by the update at timestamp and the newest are the topmost. And well, right now there's only the 100 topmost ones are listed. I didn't add the paging, there's one of the issues that I opened, but sort of my"], [0, "understanding or the way I think is that if there is a project that hasn't been updated for two years, then it's quite unlikely that if you send a pull request for anything basically, that pull request will be accepted, right? But if a project was released just a year,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["rust", "cargo", "workspace", "crates", "monorepo", "toml", "repository url", "configuration management", "copy paste", "automation", "cratesio", "database dump", "issue tracking", "metadata", "ownership", "pagination", "open source", "maintainability"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
