, "luke palmisano", "parks art and culture committee", "avison young", "berkadia", "matthews", "colorado"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778713653.4074569, "duration_sec": 1.8016209602355957, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And so I don't consider myself all that smart, but what I do consider myself is sort of just being excited by ideas. And I wish people would ask me about how to build something or how to be excited by an idea more. What can they do to build something? That's what I wish people would ask me more about."], [0, "It's interesting that you are focused on this kind of fuel, the motivation, rather than a specific skill set. All the way on the other end of that scale, I guess, would be tricks. What is the secret to a successful business? But it seems that you are kind of on the opposite end."], [0, "You're not talking about skills or tricks or that one thing that you need to do or that one book you need to read. But instead, it seems that what you care most about is this passion or fuel or focus on the idea of"], [0, "Uh, would you say that that's the most important thing for a successful venture like this? Absolutely. I would, I feel like it's easy to get caught up in everything but the idea. Um, I know that I was, I tend to have these hobbies and I tend to go deep into these hobbies. Um, I never really am successful in a hobby. For example, my most recent hobby is miniature painting. Um,"], [0, "I love board games and I tend to have lots of board games with minis and I really wanted to paint them all. So I did a lot of research in the paints that I need, the brushes that I'll need. You know, I would watch lots of YouTube videos of techniques, but in all those things that I was doing, the one thing I wasn't doing was painting. I was sort of gearing myself up. I'm like, one day I'm going to just going to learn everything I need to learn and then I'll go paint a mini."], [0, "I think I've painted two minis and I have probably a hundred paints and 20 different brushes. And I've watched probably a hundred hours of YouTube video of people painting, but I've only painted two or three minis. I feel like what I should have done is just started painting."], [0, "just start painting a mini and then I will learn. And then I could sort of hone my craft from there. And I feel like that's, you know, that's what people sometimes do with ideas for products and businesses. They skirt around the idea. They have to read this book first. They have to read this language first. They have to go to this seminar. They have to join this incubator. They have to do all these things before they can actually do the idea. And I wish that they would just"], [0, "Do the idea. Just go build it. Use what you know. Go build something. Because often I feel like, you know, they say a picture is worth a thousand words. I feel like a prototype is worth a thousand words. And it's not until, you know, if I was to, when I was starting out BuildKite, I would tell people the idea."], [0, "Oh, it's CICD platform that is hybrid. And people would say to me, oh, I don't get it. Why don't you just use X, Y, Z? Just use this other thing. I don't understand exactly what you said before. Can't you just add these two things together and get the same outcome? Absolutely you can. But it wasn't until they could have got their hands on something, something tangible, that they could say, oh, I get it now. I understand what you were trying to achieve."], [0, "And I know that it's easy to have people get you down when you do things like customer research and sort of like finding, there's a whole bunch of stuff you can do around an idea that is not building the idea. I think that if you truly are passionate about the idea, you should go try and build it. And it doesn't matter if no one buys it. It doesn't matter if it doesn't sell, because I bet you that that idea will come back in five years time and that will be a successful idea."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["entrepreneurship", "motivation", "passion", "idea generation", "product building", "prototyping", "startup mindset", "learning by doing", "avoiding procrastination", "customer research", "hobbies", "miniature painting", "software platforms", "continuous integration", "hybrid systems", "technology industry", "creativity", "innovation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778713978.8268337, "duration_sec": 2.136791706085205, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And people believe in it. I do believe\u2014 I think people, like you say, people want to\u2014 if your life is fucked or whatever, they want to be able to say, okay, this is my savior. If I believe in this, it's gonna get me on the right track. 100%. And then with that With these, like, Drewski just did a skit that went viral, right? And it was like he was making fun of the megachurches and everything. But these churches, like, they give these people something to believe in, make them feel better, and they charge people. Do you think that there should be a separation? If I inspire you, if my writings or my speeches inspire you to want to do something and change your life and be more financially secure, do you think these people entitled to like, okay, almost like agencies, if I get you to work or get pitching is, should you hit me off? Or the megachurches, is this so wrong for me just to pour all the money to them? Or are they giving these people something to believe in? If that's the case, do I supposed to get a piece of that?"], [1, "I think they're preying on people's need to believe in things, right? And I think they're very predatory. And I think that's why they're flying private jets and driving Rolls-Royces and living in mega mansions on giant ranches. And they're doing it all off of donations of people that are barely getting by. That's a lot of it. It. You know, I think it's a scam that's legal. I think if we were a just and righteous society, it wouldn't be legal, right? I mean, it's\u2014 you're taking advantage of people when they need something to believe in, and you're asking for all their money. Like, I remember I was watching this guy on TV once, like, televangelists are the worst, and this guy was saying that if you are broke, you should borrow money to donate it to the church, and it will be paid back to you tenfold. That God will pay you back tenfold. And then he had all these examples of people that did it, and they would call in and say, I, I was $1,000 in debt and this and that, but I borrowed $100 and I donated it to you, and now all of a sudden I drive a Rolls-Royce. And it's all horseshit."], [0, "But those are all desperate, desperate people."], [1, "That's for people."], [0, "Those are the same people that, yeah, I'm gonna spend $30 on the lottery every day for like fucking 50 years and don't know how much. That's another scam."], [1, "Yeah, that's another scam. Not only is that a scam, here's the scam about the lottery. Not only does\u2014 like, say if everyone pumps money into the lottery, say you, you buy $100 worth of tickets and Jamie buys $100 worth of tickets and I buy $100, so there's $300 in the lottery. There's not even $300 available to win. Right. So, and then if you win, you don't get all the money. You get the money over a long period of time."], [0, "But they always give you options."], [1, "Right, right, right. But if you, if you take the second option, I mean, it's a significantly\u2014 like, here's a good example of it. Speaking of the Epstein Files, what do you mean speaking of company Zorro Trust?"], [0, "I didn't like that transition. You looked me right in my eyes and said, speaking of the Epstein Files."], [1, "Well, Epstein, we were talking about it before the podcast. No, Epstein came to see you."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["religion", "megachurches", "faith", "donations", "exploitation", "scams", "televangelists", "private jets", "lottery", "gambling", "debt", "epstein files", "financial insecurity", "wealth inequality", "media satire", "viral skits", "ethics", "consumer protection"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778714511.3470926, "duration_sec": 4.4881911277771, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "But that does not happen in debug mode because the optimizations aren't run. So if you were to try this on binary built in debug mode, I imagine you would get more crates mentioned in these panic messages. So it would work better. But I have never even checked because I don't think anyone would run a debug mode binary in production because they're just so slow."], [0, "True, but I mean, if you're running this locally, you may want to go through debug mode because like you said, there's, I guess the optimizations don't take that much longer to run, but maybe you just want to run it quickly to see if there's any issues before you keep going. If you are just doing this locally, you should just run"], [0, "cargo audit on your cargo log file and not the binary then you don't have to do anything else you don't have to involve the binary like it's literally the same information so cargo log is actually more complete"], [0, "than this heuristic detection based on panic messages, because not all creates panic. So some just don't panic. Some could panic, but the optimizer removed all of that. And some are just C code that doesn't even have the concept of panic, like OpenSSL."], [0, "which is why OpenSSL will never show up in this heuristic detection based on panic messages because it literally cannot panic. And I think that's a big problem because you have to update OpenSSL pretty frequently. And it would be nice to know that you have it outdated, but you just have to use cargo auditable for that when you build your binaries. And I don't think there is a shortcut there, or at least I haven't found it yet."], [0, "Yeah, that's a good point. I mean, is it quite clear about what's the guarantees that you provide versus what's not guaranteed? I mean, obviously, like you just said, if you run this on a binary with OpenSSL, even OpenSSL may be vulnerable, but you're not going to report on that, if I understand correctly. Yeah, so there are several moving pieces here. One is proper cargo auditable, and the other is..."], [0, "the heuristic detection based on panic messages that I'm patching up because not everyone's using Cargo Auditable. So if you're using Cargo Auditable, then in case of OpenSSL, if you are building it into the binary statically, because you can also use it as a shared library, you just try to use the OpenSSL that's already installed in your system. And if there is none, then it errors out. That's also an option."], [0, "But if it is linked statically, then in case of OpenSSL, it is detected and it is reported on."], [0, "Because OpenSSL specifically, the way Rust bindings work is there are two separate crates. One is the Rust code for interfacing with OpenSSL. And the other is just the OpenSSL source code in C. And the source code is in a separate source package."], [0, "ending with src, and the Rust bindings are in bindings package, ending with sys. So that's openssl-src and openssl-sys. So because of this split, the version of openssl code being used is visible to cargo."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["rust", "cargo", "debug mode", "compiler optimization", "panic messages", "heuristic detection", "crate dependencies", "binary analysis", "security auditing", "cargo audit", "cargo auditable", "openssl", "static linking", "shared libraries", "c code", "software supply chain", "vulnerability management", "build process"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778714527.475225, "duration_sec": 2.102409601211548, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "it has aired out, then that error sort of propagates up to the user and so on and so forth until you have some sort of boundary that catches these. So that's the kind of thing that I want to add syntax for. You know, I want to give you precise control over when and where you show data to the user. One thing talked about a lot is the best friend's name."], [0, "problem, like the missing data problem in light of mutations. And it's not just in light of mutations. You might make another query, and in the meantime, the user's best friend has changed on the back end, and so now the best friend's name is missing for some component. So basically, any query could potentially change the state of the world."], [0, "And that's fine and dandy for an app like, you know, a social media app, because if we don't know who wrote a given post, you know, written by question mark, that's okay. Like, or we just don't show that field, that's okay. But on the other hand, if you're trying to use GraphQL for something like a banking app, and we don't know how much money is in your account, or the latest price of, you know, whatever security that you own, like, that's more of a problem. And so I think the fact that"], [0, "I'll be able to add syntax level support to precisely control what kind of data we show to the user is in the end going to make GraphQL or, you know, if GraphQL is just an implementation detail, make Isograph a good choice where GraphQL is not, for certain apps where GraphQL is not. That's sort of the motivation for writing my own language. And I guess that there's like just a few other things that are like, I think,"], [0, "like fragments aren't their own fields in GraphQL. They're sort of like part of the parent object, and that's just confusing. And the big difference between client fields and fragment spreads is not only that they're pretty associated with some sort of function that's executed, but also that they are their own field. So that means that like, if you have a client field that's only executed in certain conditions, for example, if a given type matches a given interface,"], [0, "then that client field could be missing on the parent object if it didn't match. And that's just not something that's expressible in GraphQL because the fragment spread is just part of the parent object, that parent object exists, but you don't have any indication to know that, oh, I'm not allowed to read this fragment spread because the types don't match. So these are just some of the things that I think are limitations of GraphQL, and it's to no fault of the GraphQL project that it's like this."], [0, "GraphQL is many years old and there's committees, like, you know, changes slow and stuff like that. But I do think that it's exactly the kind of situation where Isograph can move faster and hopefully a lot of these changes, if they work out, end up getting upstream to GraphQL over time. That's typical, right? Because you're kind of like a small wave hitting a Titanic and trying to, you know, force it to move in another way. It's not impossible, but it's going to take some effort and some really..."], [0, "clever and ideas and also probably a lot of outside people saying, yes, we really want this to be part of standard, right? Yeah, 100%. And that's why I want people to collaborate on Isograph. There's a lot of issues that I just recently actually fleshed out and put on the GitHub, including"], [0, "good first issues and whatever amount of support people need to help out on the project, I'm here to give it. Yeah, 100%. This is a multi-person effort. I've been putting a lot of work in the last year into this. And I think that within, let's say six months to 12 months, something like that, it'll be a very legitimate choice for people building apps. And then over time, it'll become the best choice for building apps that are data-driven."], [0, "which what kind of apps aren't, I don't know, won't be the best choice for building marketing pages. I think that that niche is heavily, heavily, heavily handled by all of the sort of the mainstream frameworks. But for data-driven apps, where consistency is important and performance is important and stuff like that, I think Isograph will be the best choice, let's say, you know, a year and a half from now."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["graphql", "isograph", "missing data", "error propagation", "data consistency", "query updates", "mutations", "user interface", "banking applications", "social media", "fragments", "client fields", "language design", "syntax support", "schema evolution", "open source", "github", "collaboration"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778714684.128552, "duration_sec": 1.8645548820495605, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I'd kind of like to go back to your past, right? Because, I mean, this is some very specific knowledge that you have to know in order to make this kind of stuff. Like, how did you get started into this kind of work? Is it just kind of a hobby that turned into like a full-blown, what I would call obsession, obsession in a bad way, but really passion in your work to start off from? Oh, I have an origin story. So it goes like this. At around 2014, I was..."], [0, "So the country I was living in was implementing some online censorship and surveillance. And I was starting to look into the whole area of privacy and security for that reason. I want to understand what's happening and how it can impact me."], [0, "And I think that was also the year when the Snowden documents were coming out during the summer. So that was a fun time to get into the topic. One of the things I did fairly early on and realized that there was this new tool that came out, a fuzzer,"], [0, "really the first actually practical fuzzer that was finding real vulnerabilities it was called american fuzzy lock or a fell for short and basically spawned the current generation of fuzzers"], [0, "And I looked at the tool and I just said, okay, let me try to use it. This is a cool new tool that's supposed to find vulnerabilities. Let's see what it does. So I took the library that does"], [0, "video decoding for Firefox on Linux. And I gave the fuzzer some really base... I think I gave just one sample of what that video file is supposed to be like, which is not a good starting point for a fuzzer. Ideally, you want to kick it off with a wide selection of different valid files so that it can then discover interesting invalid states."], [0, "And if you don't do that, then it will just spend a lot of time discovering possible valid variations and probably not even discover all of them. So ideally, for good coverage, you have to give it a wide selection of different files that are valid for this format. So I did none of that. I just made a really, really small one kilobyte video."], [0, "And I took the library and really did it in the dumbest possible way, just married them. It was slow. I think I did several different mistakes because fuzzing C code is not as easy as fuzzing Rust code. CargoFuzz makes fuzzing Rust code so easy, so easy and so fast."], [0, "You just run cargo fuzz in it and you fill in a few lines, cargo fuzz run, off you go. All it takes is like five, 10 minutes. But fuzzing C code is a lot harder. And I think I'd made like two or three different mistakes when fuzzing it. So it wasn't even good by any means. And on my machine running on a single thread, it found a heap buffer overflow in like an hour."], [0, "So I was obviously concerned because heap buffer overflow is kind of the textbook example of unexploitable vulnerability, especially in the browser context. And any website I visit can play video by default, or at least could at that time. I think there are some very basic restrictions on it now. But back then, website definitely could just go and play video. And..."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["privacy", "online censorship", "surveillance", "snowden leaks", "fuzzing", "vulnerability research", "american fuzzy lop", "firefox", "linux", "video decoding", "heap overflow", "browser security", "open source", "penetration testing", "exploit development", "software engineering", "digital rights", "risk assessment"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
